WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.399
We only have one Earth.

00:00:04.399 --> 00:00:07.750
It is a global challenge.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:11.509
It is a message of urgency.

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:19.430
It is five minutes to midnight in the global
climate emergency.

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:23.680
More and more political leaders around the
world are putting the protection of the climate

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:25.200
at the top of their agenda.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.110
Most importantly: They have agreed to rapidly
cut their greenhouse gas emissions.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:37.610
That's the most complex and longest transformation
we've done.

00:00:37.610 --> 00:00:40.440
So it's a tremendous challenge.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:43.380
But it can be done.

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.360
There are many challenges and just as many
open questions on how to meet them.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:53.120
But one thing we know for sure: it is going
to cost a lot of money.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.989
In this video, we want to look at just how
much, using Germany as an example.

00:00:58.989 --> 00:01:02.610
Before we look at that bill, let’s have
a look at how expensive the climate crisis

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:13.490
already is...first worldwide – and then
we’ll take a closer look at Germany.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:19.610
The frequency and intensity of extreme weather
events has risen sharply in recent decades.

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:23.680
Forest fires in North America, southern Europe
and Siberia.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:26.270
Typhoons in Southeast Asia.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:27.920
Floods in Germany and China.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.810
Yes, they are expensive.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:35.030
Insurance companies say extreme weather events
cost more than $80 billion, that’s roughly

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:36.320
68.5 billion euros.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:38.220
In 2020 alone.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:46.070
Among the hardest hit in the last twenty years
were Puerto Rico, Myanmar, Haiti, the Philippines

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.939
and Pakistan.

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:52.770
When the damage is at its most severe in the
countries with fewest resources to cope, the

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:55.180
consequences are devastating.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:56.840
And Germany?

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:01.600
Let’s take a look at the most recent example
to make things clearer: The floods in the

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:09.130
summer of 2021 destroyed infrastructure such
as roads, bridges, and water treatment plants.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:14.280
The German Insurance Association estimates
the damage in the affected areas at 7 billion

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.790
euros.

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:21.900
And that’s just one catastrophe made worse
and more likely by climate change.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:26.529
Germany also went through several years of
drought that damaged huge areas of farmland

00:02:26.529 --> 00:02:27.529
and woodland.

00:02:27.529 --> 00:02:32.680
There are no final numbers, but forest owners
estimated the reforestation costs to have

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:39.069
been around 640 million euros and the cost
of removing the damaged trees to have been

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:41.529
around 2.1 billion euros.

00:02:41.529 --> 00:02:44.659
You think that’s bad?

00:02:44.659 --> 00:02:50.120
Brace yourself, it is time to look into what
we will be facing very soon.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:57.389
Well, in the long run non-action is much more
expensive than action.

00:02:57.389 --> 00:03:02.150
Delayed transition would be much more expensive
but also more socially unjust.

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:07.769
To understand the experts better, we need
to look at some details: With the Paris Climate

00:03:07.769 --> 00:03:12.540
Agreement, world leaders promised to keep
the average global temperature rise well below

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:19.079
2 degrees Celsius and will strive for 1.5
degrees Celsius compared to pre-Industrial

00:03:19.079 --> 00:03:23.609
Revolution-levels.

00:03:23.609 --> 00:03:27.699
Every degree of warming makes the planet less
hospitable to humans.

00:03:27.699 --> 00:03:33.760
If the world warms 1.5 degrees Celsius then
disasters like heat waves, water shortages

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.090
and crop failures hit much harder and more
often.

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:48.449
Let’s have a look at these scenarios by
the UN.

00:03:48.449 --> 00:03:53.859
Remember the 68.5 billion euros of insurance
costs because of extreme weather events from

00:03:53.859 --> 00:03:55.269
last chapter?

00:03:55.269 --> 00:03:57.219
That was from 2020.

00:03:57.219 --> 00:03:59.019
And it’s only getting worse.

00:03:59.019 --> 00:04:00.730
A lot worse.

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:02.950
There are no definite numbers yet.

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:13.120
But - scientists and experts agree action
is necessary and that leads us - to our bill.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.310
Let’s have a look at how much it might cost
us - in Germany.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:24.920
First of all, Germany already plans to achieve
climate neutrality by 2045.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:29.810
That means balancing those emissions so they
are equal to or less than the emissions that

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:33.010
get removed through the planet’s natural
absorption.

00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:37.071
Research that focused on the costs of this
transformation came to the estimate that it

00:04:37.071 --> 00:04:40.250
will cost 6 trillion euros until 2045.

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:42.530
That's a lot of money.

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:43.740
Bear with us.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:47.840
Let’s have a look at the three sectors which
are going to swallow the biggest chunk of

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:48.880
that money.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.620
Energy, industry, and transport.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:56.990
They are the most expensive to revolutionize
because they are big, emit a lot, and are

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:01.400
complicated to decarbonize.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.070
The energy sector is Germany’s biggest emitter.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:08.980
That makes sense because we all need to heat
our houses in winter and turn on the light

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:10.060
when it gets dark.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:17.010
But – here is the problem: Daily life creates
a vast amount of CO2, since most of the energy

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:21.950
production relies on fossil fuels.

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:27.760
As an incentive to use more renewable energy,
Germany introduced a CO2 price starting this

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:28.760
year.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:34.580
The deal: companies that emit CO2 due to their
production will have to pay for it.

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:37.930
Companies then pass these costs on to their
customers.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:43.970
This makes climate-damaging fuel such as gasoline
or heating oil more expensive for them.

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:52.690
In 2021, the cost of heating an average household
with heating oil rose by 158 euros...with

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:56.040
huge social repercussions.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:02.620
The CO2 prices have to be paid by all consumers
but it affects low-income households worse

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:10.620
because they have to spend more money related
to their income than rich or high-income households

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:18.340
which have a higher CO2 footprint and also
higher incomes.

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:24.940
They are not spending enough money
for this kind of CO2 price and this is socially

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:25.940
not fair. 

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:30.800
Plus: there is not yet enough environmentally
friendly energy available for everyone to

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:31.970
switch to.

00:06:31.970 --> 00:06:36.900
But there is a solution.

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:43.030
The best way to protect ourselves from rising
energy costs is to expand the use of renewables.

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:45.990
It is a vital socio-political instrument to
prevent what we call energy poverty because

00:06:45.990 --> 00:06:55.200
it ensures a stable supply and stable prices
in the future.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:59.020
And that takes us to the second sector: Germany’s
industry.

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:01.960
The automobile industry is the driving force
here.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:07.410
It is considered the backbone of Germany’s
industrial and financial economy.

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:10.270
It needs one thing above all: steel.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:16.570
Not surprisingly, cars are mostly made of
steel.

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:19.460
The conventional manufacturing of steel is
anything but

00:07:19.460 --> 00:07:21.800
environmentally friendly.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:29.139
Around 1.7 tons of CO2 emissions are produced
for every ton of crude steel.

00:07:29.139 --> 00:07:32.980
Manufacturing techniques that cause fewer
emissions are still in their infancy.

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:38.270
The German Government - actually the outgoing
one under Angela Merkel - pledged billions

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:41.230
in investment to decarbonize steel production.

00:07:41.230 --> 00:07:46.300
The cost: 13 to 35 billion euros alone in
this decade.

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:48.240
And that’s just for the steel sector.

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:56.680
It is a promising way to make Germany’s
car production more climate-friendly.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:00.139
Speaking of which: most of the transport here
is road bound.

00:08:00.139 --> 00:08:04.730
And so it’s back to cars.

00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:11.240
Transport is the last sector we focus on and
what it means cost wise.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:16.490
To cut CO2 emissions, electric vehicles are
an alternative to the good old combustion

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:17.690
engine.

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:23.040
Currently, electric cars are more expensive
to buy than conventional cars.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:28.840
But anyone who buys an electric car in Germany
for under 40,000 euros will receive a buyer’s

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:32.210
premium of 6,000 euros from the state.

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:39.349
Plus: Products based on fossil fuels like
gasoline will become more expensive.

00:08:39.349 --> 00:08:43.729
Electricity on the other hand - you remember,
we said it - has a high potential to become

00:08:43.729 --> 00:08:47.959
more affordable, due to improved production
techniques.

00:08:47.959 --> 00:08:53.050
So in the long run, electric cars will become
more affordable than cars with combustion

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:56.730
engines.

00:08:56.730 --> 00:09:00.309
There are many questions we don’t know the
answer to yet.

00:09:00.309 --> 00:09:05.480
For example: Will scientists be fast enough
in developing methods to store renewable energy

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:07.440
efficiently?

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:11.800
Will Germany find a way to make climate protection
socially just?

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:14.220
And many more questions.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:18.769
But there is one last point, which is really
important to mention.

00:09:18.769 --> 00:09:22.220
We talked about huge sums of money in this
video.

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:26.389
Economic damage to goods, to buildings, to
infrastructure.

00:09:26.389 --> 00:09:30.480
But all this doesn’t matter in the face
of some costs that are hard to put a price

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:32.649
tag on.

00:09:32.649 --> 00:09:36.339
Humans are already dying as a consequence
of climate change.

00:09:36.339 --> 00:09:40.850
According to the World Health Organisation,
climate change is already responsible for

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:47.499
150,000 deaths around the world every year...
and that figure is rising.

00:09:47.499 --> 00:09:52.040
The air pollution from burning fossil fuels
kills millions each year.

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:54.480
8.7 million to be precise.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:58.490
That’s the latest estimate from 2018.

00:09:58.490 --> 00:10:03.800
And not only humans are dying, plant and animal
species are becoming extinct at an alarming

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:05.810
rate.

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:11.060
Protecting the climate will remain one of
the greatest challenges over the coming years.

00:10:11.060 --> 00:10:13.529
And it will be expensive.

00:10:13.529 --> 00:10:18.920
But in the long run, investing more in advance
may mean spending less to repair the damage

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:19.570
later.

